<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE topic
        SYSTEM "https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/xhtml-entities.dtd">
<topic xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
       xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/topic.v2.xsd"
       title="Setting Up a Writerside Documentation Project"
       id="Setting-Up-a-Writerside-Documentation-Project">

    <tldr>
        <p>Install the Writerside plugin, create a project, write a topic in semantic XML,
           build locally, and push to GitHub.</p>
    </tldr>

    <p>
        New to Writerside? This guide walks you through the setup process:
        from installing the plugin to getting your first project on GitHub.
    </p>

    <chapter title="Prerequisites" id="prerequisites">
        <p>Before you begin, make sure you have the following:</p>
        <list>
            <li>
                <a href="https://www.jetbrains.com/idea/download/">IntelliJ IDEA</a>
                (Community Edition is sufficient)
            </li>
            <li>A <a href="https://github.com">GitHub</a> account  </li>
        </list>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Step 1: Install the Writerside plugin" id="install-plugin">
        <p>
            The Writerside plugin turns IntelliJ IDEA into a full documentation environment:
            live preview, semantic markup, and local builds included.
        </p>
        <procedure>
            <step>Open IntelliJ IDEA.</step>
            <step>Select <ui-path>Plugins</ui-path> from the welcome screen,
                or go to <ui-path>File | Settings | Plugins</ui-path>.</step>
            <step>Select the <control>Marketplace</control> tab.</step>
            <step>Search for <emphasis>Writerside</emphasis>.</step>
            <step>Click <control>Install</control>, then restart the IDE when prompted.</step>
        </procedure>
        <tip>
            After the restart, you will see a <control>Writerside</control> panel
            in the left sidebar. This confirms the plugin is active.
        </tip>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Step 2: Create a new documentation project" id="create-project">
        <procedure>
            <step>Go to <ui-path>File | New Project</ui-path>.</step>
            <step>Select <control>Writerside</control> on the left, then select
                <control>Starter Project</control>, and click <control>Next</control>.</step>
            <step>Specify the name and location for your project and click <control>Create</control>.</step>
        </procedure>
        <tip>
            Writerside generates a default help instance (your documentation site) and
            a starter topic to explore the markup.
            Remove the topic from the TOC when you're done by right-clicking it and selecting
            <control>Remove from TOC</control>.
        </tip>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Step 3: Add a new topic" id="add-topic">
        <p>
            Topics are the individual pages of your documentation. Writerside supports
            both Markdown and XML. This guide uses XML topics for their semantic
            elements and content reuse capabilities.
        </p>
        <procedure>
            <step>In the <control>Table of Contents</control> pane, click <control>+</control>.</step>
            <step>Select <control>Empty XML Topic</control>.</step>
            <step>Specify a title and filename for your topic.</step>
            <step>Write your content using semantic elements. See the
                <a href="https://plugins.jetbrains.com/plugin/20158-writerside/docs/semantic-markup-reference.html">Semantic markup reference</a> for the full list.</step>
        </procedure>
        <tip>
            The preview opens automatically when you first open a topic. To reopen it,
            right-click any topic in the Table of Contents and select
            <control>Preview Topic</control>.
        </tip>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Step 4: Build the documentation locally" id="local-build">
        <p>
            Run a local build to catch errors before publishing.
        </p>
        <procedure>
            <step>In the <control>Writerside</control> tool window, select your instance,
                click <control>Export To</control>, and select <control>Web Archive</control>.</step>
            <step>Click <control>Run</control>.</step>
            <step>After the build completes, check the <control>Run</control> tool window
                for errors and warnings.</step>
            <step>Click <control>Show generated files</control> in the bottom-right popup
                to open the output directory. Extract the ZIP archive and open your topic's
                HTML file in a browser to preview it.</step>
        </procedure>
        <tip>
            The <control>Show generated files</control> popup appears only immediately
            after the build. If you missed it, run the build again.
        </tip>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Step 5: Push to GitHub" id="push-to-github">
        <p>
            Push your source to GitHub to keep it version-controlled and shareable.
        </p>
        <procedure>
            <step>
                In IntelliJ IDEA, go to
                <ui-path>Git | GitHub | Share Project on GitHub</ui-path>,
                or <ui-path>VCS | Share Project on GitHub</ui-path> if Git is not yet enabled.
            </step>
            <step>Enter a repository name, uncheck <control>Private</control>, and click <control>Add account</control>
                to authenticate with GitHub.</step>
            <step>Click <control>Share</control>.
                In the <control>Add Files For Initial Commit</control> dialog,
                uncheck the <path>.idea</path> folder, then click <control>Add</control>. </step>
        </procedure>
    </chapter>

</topic>